WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.581
Your criteria will move, and that’s expected.

2
00:00:02.861 --> 00:00:09.709
Shreya Shankar and colleagues studied people building LLM evaluators in “Who Validates the Validators?

3
00:00:09.989 --> 00:00:11.547
” (2024).

4
00:00:11.827 --> 00:00:24.285
Their tool, EvalGen, proposes candidate graders (code or LLM prompts) and asks the user to grade a sample of outputs, then picks the graders that best agree with the user’s grades.

5
00:00:24.565 --> 00:00:32.843
The striking finding was criteria drift: people need criteria to grade outputs, but grading outputs changes their criteria.

6
00:00:33.123 --> 00:00:37.475
Some criteria only appeared once participants saw particular outputs.

7
00:00:37.755 --> 00:00:39.824
Two practical consequences follow.

8
00:00:40.104 --> 00:00:48.253
First, a rubric written entirely before reading outputs will be incomplete, which is one more argument for doing error analysis first.

9
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:51.285
Second, your taxonomy is a living document.

10
00:00:51.565 --> 00:00:58.520
Version it, note when a definition changes, and expect to revise it when the product, the users, or the model changes.

11
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.771
Re-labeling a few old traces after a definition change keeps your counts comparable.
